\ 5{r}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{24}{5} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{30}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $r<0.$ Therefore, $r=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{30}}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bff08bf-0035-5f05-9607-493b301e8717" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given Two Perpendicular Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b27be4c-f581-5458-a959-2633136524b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b27be4c-f581-5458-a959-2633136524b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497678} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ff7f755-1ffc-5fd4-8059-46a3459e8c06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d00137d5-07f7-5c87-9146-2b0f0101752e" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨{x}^{2},-1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,2⟩.$ If $a⊥b$ and $x<0,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We know that $a⊥b$ ($a$ and $b$ are perpendicular) if and only if $a\\cdot b=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $x<0.$ Therefore, $x=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bff08bf-0035-5f05-9607-493b301e8717" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given Two Perpendicular Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d1acb72-e017-5d70-8eb9-4fdfdb674352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d1acb72-e017-5d70-8eb9-4fdfdb674352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497679} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ca76dfd-af74-5089-be0d-ba18af8c7da4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e46cbde8-81fd-5138-9b25-b9d9d745ebf8" :question/problem "Let $g=[\\begin{aligned}4{r}^{2} \\\\ -4 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]$ and $h=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Given that $g⊥h$ and $r<0$, find the value of $r.$\n\n$r=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We know that $g⊥h$ ($g$ and $h$ are perpendicular) if and only if $g\\cdot h=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & ={g}_{x}\\cdot {h}_{x}+{g}_{y}\\cdot {h}_{y}+{g}_{z}\\cdot {h}_{z} \\\\  & =4{r}^{2}\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 3{r}^{2}+(-9)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16{r}^{2}-12{r}^{2}-18 \\\\  & =4{r}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & =0 \\\\ 4{r}^{2}-18 & =0 \\\\ 4{r}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $r<0.$ Therefore, $r=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bff08bf-0035-5f05-9607-493b301e8717" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given Two Perpendicular Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42db0ff9-268e-5f06-9ee9-a7a292ada277" :answer/type {: