rac{9}{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $r>0.$ Therefore, $r=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bff08bf-0035-5f05-9607-493b301e8717" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given Two Perpendicular Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "338258b5-5456-514c-8754-93cf8061091f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497654} {:answer/id #uuid "38237f4f-5129-5256-b140-edb70815c42b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497656} {:answer/id #uuid "714a3214-aa60-50f0-aade-4841169775f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497668} {:answer/id #uuid "c06c6172-5411-5aa2-9336-178c2ad14341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497684} {:answer/id #uuid "cec53d7c-f38a-5d49-be8a-32db3b6b3e54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "714a3214-aa60-50f0-aade-4841169775f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497668} :answer-field/id #uuid "14f6fb08-46af-5637-b16c-c9f11df73b8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff91a7cd-801d-551f-898a-f82e597274c3" :question/problem "Let $g=[\\begin{aligned}4{r}^{2} \\\\ -3 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]$ and $h=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Given that $g⊥h$ and $r>0$, find the value of $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $g⊥h$ ($g$ and $h$ are perpendicular) if and only if $g\\cdot h=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & ={g}_{x}\\cdot {h}_{x}+{g}_{y}\\cdot {h}_{y}+{g}_{z}\\cdot {h}_{z} \\\\  & =4{r}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 2{r}^{2}+(-10)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12{r}^{2}-6{r}^{2}-20 \\\\  & =6{r}^{2}-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g\\cdot h & =0 \\\\ 6{r}^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ 6{r}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $r>0.$ Therefore, $r=\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{3}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given Two Perpendicular Vectors"}]}
